### How does Event Storming work?

Event Storming works backwards from events. You gather domain experts and developers and cover a wall with sticky notes.

```text
Event Storming wall at National Bank of India:

  COMMAND (blue)     AGGREGATE (yellow)    EVENT (orange)
  ─────────────      ────────────────      ──────────────────
  TransferMoney  ->  Account           ->  MoneyTransferred
  PayEmi         ->  LoanAccount       ->  EmiPaid
  BlockCard      ->  CreditCard        ->  CardBlocked
  CheckFraud     ->  FraudScore        ->  FraudDetected

Clusters of related events reveal bounded contexts:
  Account + MoneyTransferred + BalanceUpdated -> Payment Context
  LoanAccount + EmiPaid + LoanSanctioned      -> Lending Context
```

### How do you identify aggregates from Event Storming?

The thing that owns an invariant reveals an aggregate. An aggregate is a cluster of objects treated as one unit for data changes, accessed only through its root.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.Entity;
import jakarta.persistence.Id;
import jakarta.persistence.Version;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
public class Account {

    @Id
    private String accountId;
    private BigDecimal balance;
    @Version
    private Long version;  // Optimistic locking

    private final transient List<DomainEvent> uncommittedEvents = new ArrayList<>();

    public void debit(BigDecimal amount) {
        if (balance.compareTo(amount) < 0) {
            throw new IllegalStateException(
                "Insufficient funds in " + accountId);
        }
        balance = balance.subtract(amount);
        uncommittedEvents.add(new AccountDebited(accountId, amount));
    }

    public void credit(BigDecimal amount) {
        balance = balance.add(amount);
        uncommittedEvents.add(new AccountCredited(accountId, amount));
    }

    // All changes go through the root. Root enforces invariants.
    // Never go negative: this is the aggregate's invariant.
}
```

```text
Aggregate root = Account
Invariant = balance >= 0
All changes go through account.debit() and account.credit().
```

### Why must aggregates be small? (Rule 1)

A god aggregate becomes a single contended row that serializes writes under optimistic locking.

```java
package com.bank.antipattern;

import jakarta.persistence.Entity;
import jakarta.persistence.Id;
import jakarta.persistence.Version;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Map;

// WRONG: God aggregate. Every account is a field in one object.
@Entity
public class GodLedger {
    @Id
    private String ledgerId;       // only one row in the whole table
    @Version
    private Long version;           // every write contends on this version
    private Map<String, BigDecimal> accounts;

    // Two transfers on two different accounts both load version 7.
    // Both try to save version 8.
    // One wins, one gets OptimisticLockException and retries.
    // At high TPS, almost every write collides.
}

// CORRECT: Small per-account aggregate.
@Entity
public class SmallAccount {
    @Id
    private String accountId;      // each account is its own row
    @Version
    private Long version;           // contention only on same-account writes
    private BigDecimal balance;

    // Two transfers on different accounts NEVER contend.
    // Throughput scales with number of accounts.
}
```

```text
God aggregate: 1 row, every write contends -> throughput collapses
Small aggregate: N rows, contention only on same account -> scales linearly
```

### Why is the aggregate the transaction boundary? (Rule 2)

One transaction modifies exactly one aggregate and keeps its invariant true. Cross-aggregate operations use Sagas.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class AccountService {

    private final AccountRepository repository;

    public AccountService(AccountRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void debit(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        Account account = repository.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();
        account.debit(amount);  // enforces invariant: balance >= 0
        repository.save(account);
        // One transaction, one aggregate, one invariant check.
    }
    // Debiting Rajesh AND crediting Priya = two aggregates = Saga, not one tx.
}
```

```text
Rule: one transaction modifies exactly one aggregate.
Strongly consistent INSIDE one aggregate.
Eventually consistent ACROSS aggregates (Saga + events).
```

### Why reference by ID only? (Rule 3)

Inside the Transfer aggregate, store fromAccountId and toAccountId as strings, never a direct Account object reference.

```java
package com.bank.payment.model;

import jakarta.persistence.Entity;
import jakarta.persistence.Id;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Entity
public class Transfer {

    @Id
    private String transferId;
    private String fromAccountId;  // ID only, NOT @ManyToOne Account
    private String toAccountId;    // ID only, NOT @ManyToOne Account
    private BigDecimal amount;
    private String status;

    // By referencing by ID only:
    // 1. Object graph stays small (no lazy loading surprises)
    // 2. No accidental locking of the counterparty aggregate
    // 3. Account aggregate can be extracted to its own service later
    //    with NO code change to this reference.
}
```

```text
WRONG: @ManyToOne Account fromAccount;
  -> Loads and potentially locks Account aggregate.
  -> Cannot extract Account to another service without code change.

CORRECT: String fromAccountId;
  -> Small, clean reference.
  -> Extractable to separate service with zero code change.
```

### How does optimistic locking work?

Optimistic locking with @Version detects concurrent modification.

```text
Optimistic locking scenario:
  Thread A: reads Account SAV2001, version=7
  Thread B: reads Account SAV2001, version=7

  Thread A: saves with version=8 -> SUCCESS (version 7 -> 8)
  Thread B: saves with version=8 -> FAIL (expected 7, found 8)
  Thread B: catches OptimisticLockException -> retries

With small aggregate: only same-account writes contend.
With god aggregate: ALL writes contend on the same version.
```

### How do the three rules relate to service extraction?

Small aggregates with ID-only references are clean enough to extract into separate services with minimal code changes.

```text
Before extraction:
  One monolith with Account and Transfer aggregates.
  Transfer.fromAccountId = "SAV2001" (string reference)

After extraction:
  Account Service owns Account aggregate.
  Payment Service owns Transfer aggregate.
  Transfer.fromAccountId = "SAV2001" (unchanged!)
  Payment Service calls Account Service by ID.
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Aggregate size is a direct throughput lever. Smaller aggregates mean less contention, more parallelism, and cleaner service extraction. At high TPS, design the smallest aggregate that still protects its invariant.
